WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.750
Narrator: Scientists have studied a lot of

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:03.491
pregnant animals in space,

00:00:03.491 --> 00:00:06.906
including salamanders, fish, and rats,

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:08.772
but not humans.

00:00:08.772 --> 00:00:11.062
Over 60 women have traveled to space,

00:00:11.062 --> 00:00:13.616
yet none were pregnant during the trip,

00:00:13.616 --> 00:00:17.275
let alone gave birth while
floating in zero gravity.

00:00:17.275 --> 00:00:20.706
But with talk of future space
colonies and cities on Mars,

00:00:20.706 --> 00:00:22.917
there's a good chance that one day

00:00:22.917 --> 00:00:25.625
humans will give birth
somewhere beyond Earth,

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:27.983
and that brings up some
interesting questions.

00:00:27.983 --> 00:00:29.612
What will it be like?

00:00:29.612 --> 00:00:32.015
How will space babies look?

00:00:32.015 --> 00:00:36.622
And ultimately, how will giving
birth in space be different?

00:00:36.622 --> 00:00:40.080
The most obvious difference is
the low-gravity environment,

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.831
and without the aid of
Earth's gravitational pull,

00:00:42.831 --> 00:00:45.359
it might make it more
difficult for the mother

00:00:45.359 --> 00:00:47.944
when it comes time to push the baby out.

00:00:47.944 --> 00:00:51.201
Plus, if one day women
lived in space permanently,

00:00:51.201 --> 00:00:52.593
some of the risks of pregnancy

00:00:52.593 --> 00:00:54.972
would be much greater than on Earth.

00:00:54.972 --> 00:00:57.395
For one, without the
stress of Earth's gravity,

00:00:57.395 --> 00:00:59.333
her bones lose density.

00:00:59.333 --> 00:01:01.724
Studies show that
astronauts, for instance,

00:01:01.724 --> 00:01:03.981
lose 1% to 2% of their bone density

00:01:03.981 --> 00:01:06.586
for every month spent in space,

00:01:06.586 --> 00:01:09.648
and that would be especially
concerning for giving birth

00:01:09.648 --> 00:01:12.633
because the pelvis could
fracture in the process.

00:01:12.633 --> 00:01:15.954
In fact, doctors recommend
that women with brittle bones

00:01:15.954 --> 00:01:18.315
avoid a natural birth altogether,

00:01:18.315 --> 00:01:19.780
which could mean births in space

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:21.747
would be left to other methods.

00:01:22.739 --> 00:01:23.572
Scott Solomon: That could lead to

00:01:23.572 --> 00:01:25.971
an increased reliance on C-sections

00:01:25.971 --> 00:01:28.706
for childbirth for people living in space.

00:01:28.706 --> 00:01:29.956
Narrator: That's Scott Solomon,

00:01:29.956 --> 00:01:33.667
an evolutionary biologist and
professor at Rice University.

00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:35.653
He walked us through what space might do

00:01:35.653 --> 00:01:39.567
to generations of humans born
in low-gravity environments.

00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:40.710
We already know that the way

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:43.188
we give birth influences our anatomy.

00:01:43.188 --> 00:01:44.654
For example, the size of our heads

00:01:44.654 --> 00:01:47.866
is restricted by the size of
our mothers' birth canals.

00:01:47.866 --> 00:01:50.908
Solomon: With more
C-sections, that could lead to

00:01:50.908 --> 00:01:53.333
larger heads in our descendants

00:01:53.333 --> 00:01:54.849
because they wouldn't be constrained

00:01:54.849 --> 00:01:56.697
by the size of the birth canal.

00:01:56.697 --> 00:01:58.854
Narrator: And down the road, larger heads

00:01:58.854 --> 00:02:01.901
wouldn't be the only major change.

00:02:01.901 --> 00:02:05.188
We may also change color, says Solomon.

00:02:05.188 --> 00:02:07.364
That's because in space
we have less protection

00:02:07.364 --> 00:02:10.829
against harmful space
radiation like cosmic rays,

00:02:10.829 --> 00:02:13.877
so to counteract that, we may evolve

00:02:13.877 --> 00:02:17.177
Solomon: new types of skin
pigments, like the melanin

00:02:17.177 --> 00:02:20.936
that protects our skin from
ultraviolet sunlight on Earth.

00:02:20.936 --> 00:02:23.835
That could mean that future
generations living beyond Earth

00:02:23.835 --> 00:02:25.976
will evolve to have different skin colors.

00:02:25.976 --> 00:02:27.234
Narrator: The more melanin you have,

00:02:27.234 --> 00:02:28.921
the darker your skin.

00:02:28.921 --> 00:02:31.479
So Solomon predicts that
people living in space

00:02:31.479 --> 00:02:34.499
may develop darker skin over time.

00:02:34.499 --> 00:02:37.989
But he says these changes might
take centuries or millennia

00:02:37.989 --> 00:02:40.268
for enough time for many generations

00:02:40.268 --> 00:02:42.744
of women to give birth in space.

00:02:42.744 --> 00:02:44.372
Solomon: Eventually,
people living in space

00:02:44.372 --> 00:02:47.261
could evolve to be different
enough from people on Earth

00:02:47.261 --> 00:02:50.576
that we would consider them
to be different species.

00:02:50.576 --> 00:02:52.346
Narrator: But for now, we just need

00:02:52.346 --> 00:02:55.463
one very brave woman to pave the path.

